      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs12.md #2, 2026-08-06): f¨ on an
      ⍝ empty nested array whose (disclosed) prototype item has more
      ⍝ than one element used to build a result with uninitialized
      ⍝ Cells (aborts via Cell::init_other()'s assert, or dispatches a
      ⍝ garbage vtable call with asserts off). ScalarFunction.cc's
      ⍝ do_eval_fill_B() only ever wrote cell 0 of the fill result,
      ⍝ regardless of how many cells the (possibly non-scalar)
      ⍝ prototype item actually needed.
      ⍝
      Z1←+¨ 0⍴⊂⍳3
      ⍴⍴Z1
1
      ⍴Z1
0

      Z2←-¨ 0⍴⊂⍳3
      ⍴Z2
0

      ⍝ previously-working single-element-prototype case: still correct
      ⍝
      Z3←+¨0⍴⊂1
      ⍴Z3
0

      ⍝ Bugs28 #35 regression: (F⍤y)¨B (and other derived functions
      ⍝ whose operand is a defined/⎕FX'd function) with an empty B
      ⍝ returned a non-empty scalar/vector instead of an empty array.
      ⍝ Root cause: the empty-argument fill-function branch called
      ⍝ LO->eval_B(*first_B) whenever LO wasn't itself is_defined(),
      ⍝ and returned that call's result token unchanged when its class
      ⍝ wasn't TC_VALUE. For a derived LO wrapping a defined function,
      ⍝ eval_B() pushes a real SI frame and returns TOK_SI_PUSHED, so
      ⍝ the *result of running that frame* silently became the result
      ⍝ of the whole ¨ expression. Fixed in Bif_OPER1_EACH.cc by
      ⍝ treating LO->may_push_SI() the same as LO->is_defined() (both
      ⍝ mean: the fill function is the identity function, i.e. B
      ⍝ itself) -- except for ⎕EC, which also may_push_SI() but has
      ⍝ its own dedicated fabricated fill value.
      ⍝
      ⎕FX 'Z←ZZ35F B' 'Z←B+1'
ZZ35F

      Z←(ZZ35F⍤1)¨⍬ ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0⍴0

      Z←(ZZ35F⍤1)¨0⍴⊂1 2 3 ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0⍴(⊂3⍴0 0 0)

      Z←(ZZ35F⍤0)¨⍬ ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0⍴0

      Z←(ZZ35F¨)¨⍬ ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0⍴0

      Z←({⍵+1}⍤1)¨⍬ ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0⍴0

      Z←({⍵+1}¨)¨⍬ ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0⍴0

      Z←(ZZ35F⍣2)¨⍬ ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0⍴0

      ⎕FX 'Z←ZZ35G LO' 'Z←LO+1'
ZZ35G

      Z←(ZZ35G/)¨⍬ ⋄ 2 ⎕TF 'Z'
Z←0⍴0

      )ERASE ZZ35F ZZ35G

      ⍝ Bugs30 #1 regression: LO¨ as a selective-assignment target.
      ⍝
      ⍝ A NULL lvalue (an over-take of an empty vector, 1↑V for empty
      ⍝ V) used to segfault Bif_OPER1_EACH::do_eval_LB instead of
      ⍝ erroring cleanly; fixed by guarding dest==0 the same way the
      ⍝ dyadic branch now does (see Each_dyadic.tc). No display after
      ⍝ these: a selective assignment produces none (LRM p.41).

      V←⍬
      (,¨1↑V)←,1
INDEX ERROR
      (,¨1↑V)←,1
       ^
      (⌽¨1↑V)←,1
INDEX ERROR
      (⌽¨1↑V)←,1
       ^
      (⊂¨1↑V)←,1
INDEX ERROR
      (⊂¨1↑V)←,1
       ^

      ⍝ leaves 3 )SI entries for the failed immediate executions above.
      ⍝
      )SIC

      ⍝ a primitive (structural) LO must still work, per src/Value.cc's
      ⍝ own doc-comment example for LO¨ as a selective-assignment
      ⍝ target.

      V←'v' 'vw' 'vwx' 'vwxyz'
      (2↑¨V)←5
      4 ⎕CR V
┏→━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┓
┃5 ┏→━━┓ ┏→━━━━┓ ┏→━━━━━━┓┃
┃  ┃5 5┃ ┃5 5 x┃ ┃5 5 xyz┃┃
┃  ┗∼━━┛ ┗+━━━━┛ ┗+━━━━━━┛┃
┗ϵ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━┛

      ⍝ a defined LO must never see a selective-assignment target's
      ⍝ live LvalCell (it could smuggle it out into unrelated storage
      ⍝ via an ordinary side effect and leave a dangling reference
      ⍝ once the original array is freed); reject it up front instead.
      ⍝ Before this guard, an identity dfn like F here would silently
      ⍝ "work" by forwarding the LvalCell unchanged -- that accident
      ⍝ is exactly the hole being closed, so it must now be SYNTAX
      ⍝ ERROR regardless of what F does.

      FLAM←{⍵}
      W←1 2 3
      (FLAM¨W)←9 9 9
SYNTAX ERROR
      (FLAM¨W)←9 9 9
       ^       ^

      ⍝ leaves an )SI entry for the failed immediate execution above.
      ⍝
      )SIC
      )ERASE FLAM

      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs31 #4): (∊¨0↑V)←9 used to print a
      ⍝ C++ stack trace and exit(0) instead of behaving like the non-Each
      ⍝ control (∊0↑V)←9 -- selecting 0 items via an empty selective-
      ⍝ assignment target is a no-op on V, exactly like (V[⍬])←1 2 3,
      ⍝ regardless of whether the selecting function is wrapped in Each.
      ⍝ Root cause: EACH's own empty-B handling substitutes a synthetic,
      ⍝ non-lvalue prototype for its cellrefs before evaluating LO on it,
      ⍝ purely to determine LO's result shape/type -- Bif_F12_ELEMENT's
      ⍝ own ∊ hit an internal FIXME on that prototype. Like APL2, a
      ⍝ selective-specification expression is itself an ordinary
      ⍝ expression whose value is the (here, unselected) right argument,
      ⍝ even when the specification selects nothing: X←(∊0↑V)←9 gives
      ⍝ X≡9, not something derived from the empty selection.
      ⍝
      V←1 2 3
      (∊0↑V)←9          ⍝ non-Each control: already a no-op
      V
1 2 3
      X←(∊0↑V)←9        ⍝ the expression's own value is still 9
      X
9

      V←1 2 3
      (∊¨0↑V)←9         ⍝ same no-op, through Each
      V
1 2 3
      X←(∊¨0↑V)←9
      X
9

      ⍝ regression (Blake McBride, Bugs31 #22): a bracket-axis primitive
      ⍝ as the LO of Each lost its selectivity -- ⌽[1] (no LO/RO of its
      ⍝ own, just a plain function bound to an axis) is itself a
      ⍝ DerivedFunction, and DerivedFunction::get_selectivity() reported
      ⍝ SEL_NONE for it (falling through past the Each-only forwarding
      ⍝ case just above), discarding Bif_F12_ROTATE.hh's real
      ⍝ SEL_MON_X/SEL_DYA_X. Each's own selectivity check then rejected
      ⍝ (⌽[1]¨V)←B as if ⌽[1] could never be a valid selector, even
      ⍝ though both the non-axis Each and the axis form without Each
      ⍝ already accept the identical target.
      ⍝
      V←(2 2⍴⍳4)(2 2⍴⍳4)
      (⌽[1]¨V)←(2 2⍴4 3 2 1)(2 2⍴8 7 6 5)
      V
 2 1   6 5 
 4 3   8 7 

      V←(2 2⍴⍳4)(2 2⍴⍳4)
      (⊖[1]¨V)←(2 2⍴4 3 2 1)(2 2⍴8 7 6 5)
      V
 2 1   6 5 
 4 3   8 7 

      V←(2 2⍴⍳4)(2 2⍴⍳4)
      (1↑[1]¨V)←(1 2⍴9 9)(1 2⍴8 8)
      V
 9 9   8 8 
 3 4   3 4 

      ⍝ controls: the non-axis Each, and the axis form without Each,
      ⍝ already worked and must be unaffected
      ⍝
      V←(2 2⍴⍳4)(2 2⍴⍳4)
      (⌽¨V)←(2 2⍴4 3 2 1)(2 2⍴8 7 6 5)
      V
 3 4   7 8 
 1 2   5 6 

      V←(2 2⍴⍳4)(2 2⍴⍳4)
      (⌽[1]V)←(2 2⍴4 3 2 1)(2 2⍴8 7 6 5)
      V
 8 7   4 3 
 6 5   2 1 

      )ERASE V
